  S1["<div style='text-align:left;line-height:1.2'><b>1 · Job position</b><br/>Site Reliability Engineer (SRE): software engineers who run services<br/>and build systems that replace manual operations work</div>"]
  S2["<div style='text-align:left;line-height:1.2'><b>2 · Organization and workload</b><br/>Google; example services: Search, Ads, Gmail, Photos<br/>Hundreds of on-call rotations; SRE is separate from product teams</div>"]
  S3["<div style='text-align:left;line-height:1.2'><b>3 · Operating system</b><br/>Linux kernel underpins much of Google production<br/>Internal Linux distribution; gLinux Rodete (Debian-based) for employee workstations<br/>No source states which OS SREs use on their workstations<br/>Borg: cluster OS that runs huge numbers of Linux containers</div>"]
  S4["<div style='text-align:left;line-height:1.2'><b>4 · Hardware and infrastructure</b><br/>Google-designed datacenters: custom power, cooling, network and compute<br/>Jupiter Clos network inside datacenters; B4 backbone between them<br/>Thousands of machines and disks failed per cluster in a typical year</div>"]
  S5["<div style='text-align:left;line-height:1.2'><b>5 · Software, tools and applications</b><br/>Blaze/Bazel hermetic builds for C++, Java, Python, Go, JavaScript<br/>Google Front End (GFE) terminates and routes traffic to most services<br/><i>Practice lab (assumption): Ubuntu VM, Git, Bash/Python, Docker, Prometheus, minikube</i></div>"]
  S6["<div style='text-align:left;line-height:1.2'><b>6 · Required knowledge and skills</b><br/>Reliability practice: SLOs, toil limit of 50%, blameless postmortems<br/>OS concepts: scheduling, virtual memory, namespaces and cgroups<br/><i>Hands-on preparation (assumption): Linux shell, containers, metrics, incident write-up</i></div>"]
